// engine/effects/palette.emp — the per-section palette load, and Phase 2's home for
// the palette engine's composition pipeline.
//
// Split out of engine/system/buffers.emp (which keeps the CRAM upload machinery) so
// the effects suite owns its own modules, per the design's file structure. Pure code
// motion: the proc below is byte-for-byte what shipped in chain 105.
//
// Design: docs/superpowers/2026-08-11-effects-suite-design.md §5.
module engine.effects.palette in palette

use engine.structs.{Sec}

// ===========================================================================
// PER-SECTION PALETTE LOAD (effects P1)
//
// `Sec.sec_pal` finally gets a consumer. It has been a reserved descriptor field
// since the section struct was written — before this, game code hand-poked
// Palette_Buffer (see games/sonic4/test/*), so "each section has its own palette"
// was descriptor-only.
//
// The level owns CRAM lines 1-3; line 0 belongs to the character system. See the
// proc comment below — getting that boundary wrong reverts the active character's
// palette on every section crossing.
//
// P1 is an INSTANT snap. The palette engine's composition pipeline (base ->
// cycling -> cross-fade -> operators -> variants) is Phase 2; this proc becomes
// its base-layer step rather than being replaced.
//
// Note the ownership boundary: engine.buffers owns the CRAM *upload* (Palette_Buffer,
// the dirty mask, Enqueue_Dirty_Buffers). This module owns what goes IN it. Phase 2's
// composition pipeline (base -> cycling -> cross-fade -> operators -> variants) grows
// here, with the proc below becoming its base-layer step rather than being replaced.
// ===========================================================================

// -----------------------------------------------
// Palette_LoadSection — load a section's palette (CRAM lines 1-3) and mark those
// three lines dirty, so Enqueue_Dirty_Buffers DMAs them in the same VBlank.
//   a0 = Sec*   (preserved — the boundary hook still needs it)
// A NULL sec_pal means "keep current", matching the descriptor 0-convention that
// sec_parallax_config uses.
//
// `sec_pal` is 96 bytes = CRAM lines 1-3, NOT a full 128-byte 4-line image.
// **CRAM line 0 is the CHARACTER line and this proc must never touch it.**
// Player_ApplyCharacter owns it via CharacterDef.cd_palette (per-character: Sonic
// and Tails share one, Knuckles has his own) and writes it only on a character
// change. A section load that included line 0 would revert the active character's
// colours on every boundary crossing — i.e. Knuckles would turn back into Sonic
// mid-act. The level owning 1-3 and the character owning 0 is also why the OJZ
// palette data has always been exactly 96 bytes.
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc Palette_LoadSection (a0: u32) clobbers(d0-d1/a1-a2) {
        move.l  Sec.sec_pal(a0), d0
        beq.s   .keep
        movea.l d0, a1
        lea     Palette_Buffer + $20, a2    // CRAM line 1 base — line 0 is the character's
        moveq   #(96 / 4) - 1, d1
    .copy:
        move.l  (a1)+, (a2)+
        dbf     d1, .copy
        ori.b   #%1110, Palette_Dirty       // lines 1-3 only; bit 0 stays the character's
    .keep:
        rts
}
